From 6712b9e14b56730c6ccf2277312293df3f7ca15d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moto <j3tm0t0@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 19:14:33 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] SD card as a remote-drive unit after the SMB ones (C: next to
 B:), and sdcopy over UDP 6869

fileop.h sends every FUNC_* of a unit whose rootpath starts with "sd:" to sdfs.c (FatFs).
A REMOTEn slot "sd:/..." uses that directory; with no such slot, a readable card adds one
unit "sd:/" after the configured ones (vd_remoteunit() is what the Z sees: LBA 4 signature,
CMD_INIT). No SCSI ID is added, so B: stays the SMB remote drive. Set up in vd_init, without
waiting for Wi-Fi. File dates are not settable (FF_USE_CHMOD 0); "."/".." are synthesized.

All FatFs calls go through one recursive mutex (sdc_lock), since HDS I/O, the remote unit,
the boot fetch and sdcopy run in different tasks. my_rtc.c replaces time() with the
always-on timer, which stayed at 0 (libsmb2 timeouts never fired): start it at 0 in sdc_init
and set it from the SMB server time once.

sdcopy <share/path> <sd-path>: own task and own SMB connection, .part then rename, refuses
a target open as an HDS (the Z is using that SCSI ID) and a second copy while one runs.
status shows sdcopy=idle|run:N/M|done:N|err:<why>.
Handle ops (READ/WRITE/CLOSE/LSEEK/... and READDIR/CLOSEDIR) dispatch by a tag in the
handle's upper 32 bits (0xfffe5344; an SMB handle has its smb2_context there), because the
Z driver sends unit 0 with them. SDFS_LOG (off) prints FatFs failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PrGdt1XK1exBgERrMmLA1u
